{{package}}

import dev.ravel.jvm.Hook;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
{{import Minecraft}}
{{import Button}}
// The binary name is the type's name here, for the reason RavelSettingsScreen
// records: the pinned version reaches every interactive widget through a nested
// type and the stubs carry the binary name the JVM links on.
{{import Button$OnPress}}
{{import EditBox}}
{{import Screen}}
{{import Component}}

/**
 * Ravel's mod list and dependency diagnostics screen.
 *
 * <p>{@code ROADMAP.md}'s M3-W5 row asks for a mod list and diagnostics UI over
 * the model {@code ravel-loader} already holds, ordered by resolved load order,
 * and for valid and invalid graphs to render deterministic, actionable messages.
 * ADG-6 settled the drawing half before this screen existed: it names M3-W5's
 * mod list as <i>the same mechanism</i> as the settings screen rather than a
 * second one. So this is that mechanism again — one batched read on the same
 * length-prefixed framing, the same widget vocabulary, and no drawing outside
 * this class.
 *
 * <p><b>One crossing, not two.</b> A mod list is read and never written, so the
 * session makes a single call: {@code Hook.ravelModSurface} in {@link #init}.
 * Nothing crosses per row and nothing per frame.
 *
 * <p><b>The sentence is composed here.</b> A failure arrives as attribution —
 * kind, surface, dependency chain, the state it failed in, the detail — rather
 * than as text already flattened into a sentence, because flattening it on the
 * Rust side would have taken the choice of what to show away from the only part
 * of Ravel that knows what a player is looking at. The composing is
 * {@link Entry#lines}, and it is the only place a message is built.
 *
 * <p><b>Actionable and reportable are drawn differently on purpose.</b> A failed
 * required mod is actionable: the set cannot open a world without it, so it is
 * marked and counted in the summary. A failed optional mod is reported and not
 * marked, because the plan already tolerates its absence and telling a player to
 * fix it would be asking for work the loader does not need done. That is the
 * difference between a diagnostic and a nag, and it is the loader's distinction
 * rather than this screen's — {@code ModEntry.is_actionable} decides it and this
 * screen only draws what it decided.
 *
 * <p><b>A notice is drawn above the rows and counted with them.</b> The launch
 * records things a player is told without being refused — a resource pack that
 * was written and could not be switched on is the one there is — and those are
 * about the launch rather than about any one mod, so they sit above the rows
 * with no row of their own. The summary counts them, because that line is what a
 * player reads before deciding nothing is wrong. Each is split across as many
 * rows as its text needs: a notice says what happened <i>and</i> what can be
 * done about it, and an {@link EditBox} given more text than it has width draws
 * only the tail, so a notice on one row is a notice whose remedy is scrolled out
 * of sight.
 *
 * <p><b>Rows are drawn actionable-first, and every row states its load
 * position.</b> The two go together and the second is what makes the first safe.
 * The model stays in resolved load order — that is what {@code surface.rs}
 * promises and nothing here changes it — but with a row cap, drawing in strict
 * order means a large set cuts off exactly the failures this screen exists to
 * show, and a diagnostic screen that can hide the diagnostic has failed at its
 * only job. Grouping is still deterministic: the same set always draws the same
 * screen. The position is numbered on every row, not only on moved ones, so a
 * player reads the load order from the numbers rather than inferring it from
 * where a row happens to sit. Without the numbers this reordering would not be
 * defensible.
 *
 * <p><b>Known limit: no scrolling, so the window is divided rather than spent in
 * order.</b> A scroll container in the pinned version is another nested builder
 * and another stub seed, and this is the first pass rather than the last. So the
 * rows the window has, less the summary, are divided: every group with anything
 * to draw is guaranteed {@link #NOTICE_FLOOR}, {@link #MOD_FLOOR} or
 * {@link #SETTINGS_FLOOR} rows including its own "not shown" line, and what is
 * left over goes to the notices, then the rows, then the settings. No group can
 * take another's floor, and nothing is cut without being counted: a group that
 * kept a row says how many it cut, and the summary says it for every group that
 * did not, including a group the window left with no row at all. Actionable
 * entries are ordered first, so the rows a player has to act on are never cut,
 * and no window makes this offer more than {@link #MAX_ROWS} rows or
 * {@link #MAX_SETTINGS} settings entries. Seeding a scroll widget is the fix
 * when a real set outgrows this.
 *
 * <p>Every widget here is one the game builds without this class drawing
 * anything, for the reason {@link RavelSettingsScreen} records at length:
 * {@code AbstractButton} declares {@code extractContents} and implementing it
 * would be drawing, which is the one thing ADG-6 forbids. Rows are uneditable
 * text fields, which is the same choice a pack-fixed settings row makes and
 * needs no seed this repository does not already have.
 */
public final class RavelModsScreen extends {{Screen}} {

    /** The most mod rows this screen offers to draw, however tall the window is. */
    private static final int MAX_ROWS = 12;

    /** The most settings entries it offers, for the same reason. */
    private static final int MAX_SETTINGS = 4;

    /** The rows a non-empty notice block is guaranteed, one of them its own line. */
    private static final int NOTICE_FLOOR = 2;

    /** The rows the mod list is guaranteed, one of them its own line. */
    private static final int MOD_FLOOR = 3;

    /** The rows the settings entries are guaranteed, one of them their own line. */
    private static final int SETTINGS_FLOOR = 2;

    /** What marks a line as one a player has to act on. */
    private static final String ATTENTION = "[needs attention] ";

    /** The mod set as it stood when this screen opened. */
    private final List<Entry> entries = new ArrayList<>();

    /** What the launch told the player without refusing them, as it stood then. */
    private final List<String> notices = new ArrayList<>();

    /** The rows those notices were split into, as this screen last drew them. */
    private final List<String> noticeRows = new ArrayList<>();

    /** How many entries the loader says a player has to act on. */
    private int actionable;

    /** The side the set is running on, as the loader reported it. */
    private String side = "";

    /** Whether the payload read at all. */
    private boolean read;

    private RavelModsScreen() {
        super({{Component}}.{{Component#translatable}}("ravel.mods.title"));
    }

    /**
     * Builds the screen.
     *
     * <p>Called reflectively by {@code dev.ravel.jvm.Screens}, which cannot name
     * this type: {@code Screens} lives in the agent jar and ADG-1 forbids that
     * jar naming a game type, which this class's supertype is.
     *
     * @return a screen the caller hands to the game
     */
    public static {{Screen}} open() {
        return new RavelModsScreen();
    }

    /** The mod set this screen read, in resolved load order. */
    public List<Entry> entries() {
        return List.copyOf(entries);
    }

    /** How many entries a player has to act on. */
    public int actionable() {
        return actionable;
    }

    /** What the launch told the player without refusing them. */
    public List<String> notices() {
        return List.copyOf(notices);
    }

    /** The rows those notices were split into, as this screen drew them. */
    public List<String> noticeRows() {
        return List.copyOf(noticeRows);
    }

    /**
     * Reads the mod set once and builds one row per mod.
     *
     * <p>A payload that does not read leaves the list empty and says so rather
     * than drawing part of one. Half a mod list is worse than none: it looks
     * like the set is smaller than it is, which is the opposite of a diagnostic.
     */
    @Override
    protected void {{Screen#init}}() {
        super.{{Screen#init}}();
        entries.clear();
        notices.clear();
        noticeRows.clear();
        read = parse(Hook.ravelModSurface());

        List<String> lines = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> owners = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int index = 0; index < notices.size(); index++) {
            for (String line : Layout.wrap({{Screen#font}}, ATTENTION + notices.get(index))) {
                lines.add(line);
                owners.add(index);
            }
        }
        List<Entry> drawn = ordered();
        List<Entry> offered = configurable();
        int[] noticeSizes = ones(lines.size());
        int[] modSizes = new int[Math.min(drawn.size(), MAX_ROWS)];
        for (int index = 0; index < modSizes.length; index++) {
            modSizes[index] = drawn.get(index).lines().size();
        }
        int[] settingSizes = ones(Math.min(offered.size(), MAX_SETTINGS));
        boolean moreMods = drawn.size() > modSizes.length;
        boolean moreSettings = offered.size() > settingSizes.length;

        int[] given =
                share(
                        rows() - 1,
                        new int[] {
                            demand(noticeSizes, false),
                            demand(modSizes, moreMods),
                            demand(settingSizes, moreSettings),
                        });
        // A single row goes to the notice's own first line rather than to a line
        // saying the notice was cut, because the summary already says that.
        int noticesDrawn =
                given[0] == 1 && !lines.isEmpty() ? 1 : fitting(noticeSizes, given[0], false);
        int modsDrawn = fitting(modSizes, given[1], moreMods);
        int settingsDrawn = fitting(settingSizes, given[2], moreSettings);
        noticeRows.addAll(lines.subList(0, noticesDrawn));

        int modRows = 0;
        for (int index = 0; index < modsDrawn; index++) {
            modRows += modSizes[index];
        }
        int noticeRoom = given[0] - noticesDrawn;
        int modRoom = given[1] - modRows;
        int settingRoom = given[2] - settingsDrawn;

        int y = Layout.TOP;
        {{Screen#addRenderableWidget}}(
                label(
                        summary(
                                noticesDrawn == 0 ? 0 : owners.get(noticesDrawn - 1) + 1,
                                silent(drawn.size() - modsDrawn, modRoom),
                                silent(offered.size() - settingsDrawn, settingRoom)),
                        y));
        y += Layout.ROW;

        for (String line : noticeRows) {
            {{Screen#addRenderableWidget}}(label(line, y));
            y += Layout.ROW;
        }
        y = cut(lines.size() - noticesDrawn, noticeRoom, "more notice line", "more notice lines", y);

        for (int index = 0; index < modsDrawn; index++) {
            for (String line : drawn.get(index).lines()) {
                {{Screen#addRenderableWidget}}(label(line, y));
                y += Layout.ROW;
            }
        }
        y = cut(drawn.size() - modsDrawn, modRoom, "more mod", "more mods", y);

        for (int index = 0; index < settingsDrawn; index++) {
            final String owner = offered.get(index).modId;
            {{Screen#addRenderableWidget}}(
                    {{Button}}.{{Button#builder}}(
                                    {{Component}}.{{Component#literal}}("Settings: " + owner),
                                    new {{Button$OnPress}}() {
                                        @Override
                                        public void {{Button$OnPress#onPress}}({{Button}} pressed) {
                                            {{Minecraft}}.{{Minecraft#getInstance}}()
                                                    .{{Minecraft#setScreen}}(RavelMenu.openSettings(owner));
                                        }
                                    })
                            .{{Button$Builder#bounds}}(centre(), y, Layout.WIDGET_WIDTH, Layout.WIDGET_HEIGHT)
                            .{{Button$Builder#build}}());
            y += Layout.ROW;
        }
        y =
                cut(
                        offered.size() - settingsDrawn,
                        settingRoom,
                        "more settings screen",
                        "more settings screens",
                        y);
        {{Screen#addRenderableWidget}}(
                {{Button}}.{{Button#builder}}({{Component}}.{{Component#literal}}("Profiling"), new {{Button$OnPress}}() {
                            @Override
                            public void {{Button$OnPress#onPress}}({{Button}} pressed) {
                                {{Minecraft}}.{{Minecraft#getInstance}}().{{Minecraft#setScreen}}(RavelMenu.openProfiling());
                            }
                        })
                        .{{Button$Builder#bounds}}(centre(), y, Layout.WIDGET_WIDTH, Layout.WIDGET_HEIGHT)
                        .{{Button$Builder#build}}());
        y += Layout.ROW;
        {{Screen#addRenderableWidget}}(
                {{Button}}.{{Button#builder}}({{Component}}.{{Component#translatable}}("gui.done"), new {{Button$OnPress}}() {
                            @Override
                            public void {{Button$OnPress#onPress}}({{Button}} pressed) {
                                {{Screen#onClose}}();
                            }
                        })
                        .{{Button$Builder#bounds}}(centre(), y + Layout.ROW, Layout.WIDGET_WIDTH, Layout.WIDGET_HEIGHT)
                        .{{Button$Builder#build}}());
    }

    /**
     * How many rows this window has between the title and the two buttons.
     *
     * <p>Two rows are reserved below the list: Profiling and Done. Subtracting
     * them here is what keeps them on screen whatever is drawn above them.
     * {@link Layout#rows} measures the rest.
     *
     * @return at least one row, however small the window claims to be
     */
    private int rows() {
        return Layout.rows({{Screen#height}}, 2);
    }

    /**
     * How the window's rows are divided between the three groups.
     *
     * <p>Every group that has something to draw takes its floor first, in
     * priority order, and only what is left of the budget after all three is
     * handed out — again in priority order, and only to a group that got a floor.
     * Spending the budget in one pass instead is what let a seven-line notice
     * leave a window with no mod row on it at all: a group that draws first and
     * takes what it wants starves whatever draws after it, and a mod list with no
     * mods is not a mod list.
     *
     * <p>A window too small for even the floors gives each group what is left
     * when its turn comes, which can be nothing. A group with no rows cannot
     * draw its own "not shown" line, so {@link #summary} names every such group
     * — a screen that had to cut a whole group says so rather than quietly
     * showing less than it counted. A group left exactly one row spends it on
     * content, because the summary is already carrying the count.
     *
     * @param budget the rows below the summary
     * @param want what each group needs to draw everything it has
     * @return the rows each group may spend
     */
    private static int[] share(int budget, int[] want) {
        int[] floors = {NOTICE_FLOOR, MOD_FLOOR, SETTINGS_FLOOR};
        int[] given = new int[want.length];
        for (int group = 0; group < want.length; group++) {
            given[group] = Math.max(0, Math.min(Math.min(want[group], floors[group]), budget));
            budget -= given[group];
        }
        for (int group = 0; group < want.length; group++) {
            if (given[group] == 0) {
                continue;
            }
            int extra = Math.min(want[group] - given[group], budget);
            given[group] += extra;
            budget -= extra;
        }
        return given;
    }

    /**
     * How many rows a group needs to draw everything it has.
     *
     * @param sizes the rows each of its units draws
     * @param beyond whether it holds units these sizes do not cover
     * @return the rows, including one for a "not shown" line it already knows it
     *     needs
     */
    private static int demand(int[] sizes, boolean beyond) {
        int rows = beyond ? 1 : 0;
        for (int size : sizes) {
            rows += size;
        }
        return rows;
    }

    /**
     * How many of a group's units fit in the rows it was given.
     *
     * <p>A unit is only drawn when the row its "not shown" line would need is
     * still free after it, which is what makes that line unconditional: a group
     * that cut anything always has the row to say so. The last unit is the
     * exception, because a group with nothing left over has nothing to say.
     *
     * @param sizes the rows each unit draws
     * @param share the rows the group was given
     * @param beyond whether it holds units these sizes do not cover
     * @return how many units to draw, from the front
     */
    private static int fitting(int[] sizes, int share, boolean beyond) {
        int used = 0;
        int count = 0;
        for (int index = 0; index < sizes.length; index++) {
            int held = index + 1 == sizes.length && !beyond ? 0 : 1;
            if (used + sizes[index] + held > share) {
                break;
            }
            used += sizes[index];
            count++;
        }
        return count;
    }

    /**
     * Draws a group's "not shown" line, if it cut anything and has the row.
     *
     * <p>The room is what is left of the group's share after its drawn units, not
     * the share itself: a group given one row and spending it on content has
     * nowhere to put this line, and {@link #summary} carries the count instead.
     *
     * @param undrawn how many units were not drawn
     * @param room the rows left over from the group's share
     * @param one the noun for one of them
     * @param many the noun for several
     * @param y where the line would sit
     * @return where the next widget sits
     */
    private int cut(int undrawn, int room, String one, String many, int y) {
        if (undrawn <= 0 || room <= 0) {
            return y;
        }
        {{Screen#addRenderableWidget}}(label(plural(undrawn, one, many) + " not shown", y));
        return y + Layout.ROW;
    }

    /**
     * How many of a group's undrawn units nothing below the summary will mention.
     *
     * <p>Zero when the group kept a row for its own "not shown" line, because two
     * lines saying the same thing read as two different cuts.
     *
     * @param undrawn how many units were not drawn
     * @param room the rows left over from the group's share
     * @return what the summary has to say itself
     */
    private static int silent(int undrawn, int room) {
        return room > 0 ? 0 : Math.max(0, undrawn);
    }

    /** One row per unit, which is what a group of single-line units draws. */
    private static int[] ones(int units) {
        int[] sizes = new int[units];
        Arrays.fill(sizes, 1);
        return sizes;
    }

    /**
     * The set with actionable entries first, each group keeping load order.
     *
     * <p>Load order is what the row asks the list to be in, and it still is
     * within each group. Putting the entries a player has to act on at the top is
     * what stops the no-scrolling limit above cutting off exactly the rows the
     * screen exists to show.
     */
    private List<Entry> ordered() {
        List<Entry> sorted = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Entry entry : entries) {
            if (entry.actionable) {
                sorted.add(entry);
            }
        }
        for (Entry entry : entries) {
            if (!entry.actionable) {
                sorted.add(entry);
            }
        }
        return sorted;
    }

    /**
     * The mods a settings screen has something to draw for, in load order.
     *
     * <p>Capped like {@link #ordered()} and for the same reason: without
     * scrolling, an entry drawn past the bottom takes Profiling and Done with it.
     * Load order rather than the actionable-first order above, because a settings
     * entry is not a diagnostic and a player looks for it by name.
     */
    private List<Entry> configurable() {
        List<Entry> offered = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Entry entry : entries) {
            if (entry.configurable) {
                offered.add(entry);
            }
        }
        return offered;
    }

    /**
     * The line above the list, saying what the player is looking at.
     *
     * <p>The count includes a notice as well as a failed mod, and it is followed
     * by what is not on the screen below it. A player who reads that three things
     * need attention and finds two on the screen has been told the screen is
     * complete, which is the one thing this line must never say when it is not.
     *
     * <p>All three groups are named here and not only the notices. A window too
     * small for every floor gives some group nothing at all, and a group with no
     * row has no way to say it was cut — so this line says it for them, and a
     * group that kept the row says it itself. An installed-nothing launch still
     * counts its notices, because a launch can have something to tell a player
     * about with no mod to hang it on.
     *
     * <p><b>Known limit: this line is one row and can outgrow it.</b> A window
     * that cut two groups needs a clause for each, and the widest text a row of
     * this screen renders whole is about fifty characters — so on the smallest
     * windows the box draws its tail and the mod count scrolls out of sight. The
     * counts are the half worth keeping, because they are the half nothing else
     * on the screen says. A wrapped summary is the fix, and it needs rows those
     * windows do not have, so it waits for the scroll container the class note
     * above already names.
     *
     * @param told how many notices are drawn below this line
     * @param mods how many mod rows nothing below this line will mention
     * @param settings how many settings entries nothing below it will mention
     * @return the line
     */
    private String summary(int told, int mods, int settings) {
        if (!read) {
            return "The mod list could not be read.";
        }
        int attention = actionable + notices.size();
        StringBuilder line =
                new StringBuilder(
                        entries.isEmpty()
                                ? "No mods are installed"
                                : plural(entries.size(), "mod", "mods") + " on " + side);
        if (attention == 0) {
            line.append(entries.isEmpty() ? "." : ", none needing attention");
        } else {
            line.append(", ").append(plural(attention, "needs", "need")).append(" attention");
        }
        unshown(line, notices.size() - told, "notice", "notices");
        unshown(line, mods, "mod", "mods");
        unshown(line, settings, "settings", "settings");
        return line.toString();
    }

    /** Appends one group's "not shown" clause, when it has anything to report. */
    private static void unshown(StringBuilder line, int count, String one, String many) {
        if (count > 0) {
            line.append(", ").append(plural(count, one, many)).append(" not shown");
        }
    }

    /** One uneditable text field, which is how this screen shows a line. */
    private {{EditBox}} label(String text, int y) {
        {{EditBox}} box =
                new {{EditBox}}({{Screen#font}}, centre(), y, Layout.WIDGET_WIDTH, Layout.WIDGET_HEIGHT, {{Component}}.{{Component#literal}}(text));
        box.{{EditBox#setMaxLength}}(1024);
        box.{{EditBox#setValue}}(text);
        box.{{EditBox#setEditable}}(false);
        return box;
    }

    private int centre() {
        return ({{Screen#width}} - Layout.WIDGET_WIDTH) / 2;
    }

    /** A count and the word that agrees with it. */
    private static String plural(int count, String one, String many) {
        return count + " " + (count == 1 ? one : many);
    }

    /**
     * Reads the payload into entries.
     *
     * @return whether the whole payload read
     */
    private boolean parse(String payload) {
        if (payload == null) {
            return false;
        }
        Wire wire = new Wire(payload);
        String version = wire.field();
        if (version == null || !version.equals(Wire.MODS_VERSION)) {
            return false;
        }
        String reportedSide = wire.field();
        int reportedActionable = wire.count();
        int count = wire.count();
        if (reportedSide == null || reportedActionable < 0 || count < 0) {
            return false;
        }

        List<Entry> pending = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int index = 0; index < count; index++) {
            Entry entry = Entry.read(wire);
            if (entry == null) {
                return false;
            }
            pending.add(entry);
        }

        int noticed = wire.count();
        if (noticed < 0) {
            return false;
        }
        List<String> told = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int index = 0; index < noticed; index++) {
            String notice = wire.field();
            if (notice == null) {
                return false;
            }
            told.add(notice);
        }
        if (!wire.finished()) {
            return false;
        }

        side = reportedSide;
        actionable = reportedActionable;
        entries.addAll(pending);
        notices.addAll(told);
        return true;
    }

    /** One mod, as the loader described it. */
    public static final class Entry {

        int position;
        String modId = "";
        String state = "";
        String requirement = "";
        boolean loadsHere;
        boolean actionable;
        boolean configurable;
        boolean failed;
        String kind = "";
        String surfaceKind = "";
        String surfaceName = "";
        String failedIn = "";
        String version = "";
        List<String> chain = List.of();
        String detail = "";

        /** Reads one entry, or {@code null} if the payload runs out. */
        static Entry read(Wire wire) {
            Entry entry = new Entry();
            entry.position = wire.count();
            entry.modId = wire.field();
            entry.state = wire.field();
            entry.requirement = wire.field();
            String here = wire.field();
            String act = wire.field();
            String configurable = wire.field();
            int failures = wire.count();
            if (entry.position < 0
                    || entry.modId == null
                    || entry.state == null
                    || entry.requirement == null
                    || here == null
                    || act == null
                    || configurable == null
                    || failures < 0) {
                return null;
            }
            entry.loadsHere = here.equals("true");
            entry.actionable = act.equals("true");
            entry.configurable = configurable.equals("true");
            if (failures == 0) {
                return entry;
            }

            entry.failed = true;
            entry.kind = wire.field();
            entry.surfaceKind = wire.field();
            entry.surfaceName = wire.field();
            entry.failedIn = wire.field();
            entry.version = wire.field();
            int links = wire.count();
            if (entry.kind == null
                    || entry.surfaceKind == null
                    || entry.surfaceName == null
                    || entry.failedIn == null
                    || entry.version == null
                    || links < 0) {
                return null;
            }
            List<String> chain = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int index = 0; index < links; index++) {
                String link = wire.field();
                if (link == null) {
                    return null;
                }
                chain.add(link);
            }
            entry.chain = chain;
            entry.detail = wire.field();
            return entry.detail == null ? null : entry;
        }

        /**
         * Where this mod sits in the resolved load order, numbered from one.
         *
         * <p>Every row states it, which is what lets this screen draw actionable
         * entries first without taking the load order away from a player: the
         * order is read from the numbers rather than inferred from where a row
         * happens to sit.
         */
        public int position() {
            return position;
        }

        /** The mod id this entry describes. */
        public String modId() {
            return modId;
        }

        /** Whether a player has to act on this entry. */
        public boolean isActionable() {
            return actionable;
        }

        /**
         * The lines this entry draws, composed from its structured fields.
         *
         * <p>This is the only place in Ravel where a failure becomes a sentence.
         * Everything above it — the loader, the crossing, the wire — carries
         * attribution, so that this method can decide what a player sees without
         * anything having decided for it.
         */
        public List<String> lines() {
            StringBuilder head = new StringBuilder();
            head.append(position).append(". ").append(modId);
            if (!version.isEmpty()) {
                head.append(' ').append(version);
            }
            head.append(" — ").append(state);
            if (requirement.equals("optional")) {
                head.append(" (optional)");
            }
            if (!loadsHere) {
                head.append(" (not on this side)");
            }
            if (!failed) {
                return List.of(head.toString());
            }
            head.append(actionable ? "  [needs attention]" : "  [reported]");

            StringBuilder why = new StringBuilder("    ");
            why.append(kind.isEmpty() ? "failed in " + failedIn : kind);
            if (!surfaceKind.isEmpty()) {
                why.append(" at ").append(surfaceKind).append(' ').append(surfaceName);
            }
            if (chain.size() > 1) {
                why.append(" via ").append(String.join(" -> ", chain));
            }
            if (!detail.isEmpty()) {
                why.append(": ").append(detail);
            }
            return List.of(head.toString(), why.toString());
        }
    }
}
